# The Waitress Knew Your Order

Denise Prokop has been working the counter at the Susquehanna Diner on Fourth Street in Sunbury, Pennsylvania, since a Tuesday in the March of 1994.

She is fifty-eight years old. She wears white sneakers she buys twice a year at the Boscov's in Selinsgrove. She knows two hundred and thirty-one regular customers by first name, forty-seven of them by their order, and the eleven who have been widowed since 2019 by which side of the counter to lead them to on a Sunday morning after church.

On any given Wednesday morning at seven-forty-two, she pours a black decaf for Mr. Kepner — who lost his wife Janet in the June of 2022 — before he has taken off his coat. She does not ask him if he wants it. She has poured him a black decaf on Wednesday mornings since the July of that summer, and one week the coffee was on the counter before Mr. Kepner had even reached the door. He had waved from the parking lot. That small ritual is the reason he still comes.

Denise is, by any honest reckoning, the most gifted fundraiser I have ever met.

She has never raised a dollar.

## What Denise does that nobody notices

Denise, on a slow Tuesday afternoon between the two-thirty lull and the three-forty-five bus from the elementary school, does three things at once, without effort, and without any part of her job description acknowledging that she is doing them.

She *notices.* Mr. Kepner is quieter than usual this week. Mrs. Bierly ordered wheat toast for the first time in eight years, because her doctor changed her cholesterol prescription in the June. The teenagers in the corner booth are sharing a milkshake, which they always do when one of them has been dumped. The trucker at the counter has ordered coffee three times without ordering food, which means his rig broke down at the Turnpike stop and he is waiting on a tow.

She *remembers.* Not what the CRM would call *history.* The other kind. That Mr. Kepner and Janet used to order the same waffle, on Sundays, since 2003. That Mrs. Bierly's granddaughter is at Bloomsburg on a soccer scholarship, and that the granddaughter came home last October for the funeral. That the teenagers' mother was Denise's best friend at Susquehanna Valley High in 1986. That the trucker's father used to run the same route in an International Harvester in 1974.

She *times.* She refills the coffee before the cup is empty. She does not refill it when she can see the couple in the corner booth are having a conversation they need to have. She brings the check when the couple has stopped talking and the plates are pushed to the edge. She does not bring the check when the man is reaching, quietly, into his jacket pocket for a small folded thing he has been carrying since he left the house.

Notice. Remember. Time.

That is the whole craft.

## What the development office has been doing instead

Fifty miles east, in a converted brick building on the north side of a small college town, a mid-career associate director of major gifts named Sarah has just returned from her ten-thirty with Mr. Whitcomb. She had a script. She had a portfolio. She had a briefing document autogenerated by the CRM at 8:47 that morning — his giving history, his estimated capacity band, his last two touches, and a suggested ask amount pulled from a segmentation model built in Denver in 2014.

She did not know that his wife Louise had a stroke on the eleventh of June.

She did not know because the CRM did not know, and the CRM did not know because there is no field on Mr. Whitcomb's record where the stroke could go.

She talked, warmly and well, about the capital campaign for eighteen minutes. Mr. Whitcomb nodded. He asked one question about the campaign timeline. He looked at his phone twice. At eleven-oh-eight he said *this has been wonderful, thank you for making the time,* and stood up.

He will not take Sarah's second meeting.

He would have taken Denise's.

## What twenty years of software has quietly untaught

Every honest development office in America is, in the middle of a small tragedy nobody is quite calling by its name. The tools it has bought over the last twenty years have — one at a time, and often at annual-contract prices in the low six figures — done small damage to each part of the craft Denise does for free.

*Noticing* has been outsourced to the wealth screen. Which will tell you a donor's estimated capacity band, and will not tell you her husband is on hospice. The screen was never asked to notice. Nobody scored it on whether it noticed. So it does not.

*Remembering* has been delegated to the CRM. Which will tell you a donor's last gift amount and date, and will not remember that her granddaughter is at Bloomsburg on a soccer scholarship and came home in October for the funeral. The remembering the CRM does is the flat kind. The remembering Denise does is the round kind. They are not the same skill wearing different clothes. They are two different skills, and the field has been quietly substituting the wrong one for twenty years.

*Timing* has been handed to the marketing-automation platform. Which will send Mr. Whitcomb the second email in the spring renewal sequence on the Friday morning after his wife's stroke, because the vendor's playbook says Friday morning at 8:47 has the strongest open rate for donors between sixty-five and seventy-four in Northeastern zip codes. The platform was never taught the small brutal thing every waitress at the Susquehanna Diner learns in her first week.

*You do not refill the coffee when the couple is crying.*

The tools have done, honestly, exactly what they were asked to do. They were asked to do the wrong things.

## What a tool should quietly do here

Rōmy is not, and cannot be, Denise.

The refill is Denise's. The nod at the door is Denise's. The small quiet decision to seat Mr. Kepner on the side of the counter that does not face the booth where he used to sit with Janet is Denise's. Those are, in the end, the whole gift.

What a tool can do — quietly, on a Monday morning at the coffee pot, twenty minutes before Sarah opens the CRM — is hand her the small careful page Denise would have written if Denise were the assistant to every major-gift officer in America.

The page would say:

*Whitcomb, Charles R. Two-hundred-and-fourteen dollar sustaining since 2004. His wife Louise had a stroke on the eleventh of June — mention on the fourteenth in the community column of the* Sunbury Daily Item, *by a neighbor who happens to be on the parish council with your board chair. Their granddaughter Emma is at Bloomsburg on a soccer scholarship and came home in the October for her grandfather-in-law's funeral. Charles's own father drove the Turnpike route between Selinsgrove and Harrisburg for the Susquehanna Motor Freight from 1968 to 1991. Suggestion, this week: no ask. A plain card. Two sentences. Ask about Louise. Sign it. Mail it from your kitchen table on Saturday morning, so the postmark reads Sunbury and not the office.*

Then Sarah, on Saturday morning at the small oak desk under the west window, writes the card.

That is the whole product.

## A small assignment, with love

Sit at the counter of a diner in your service area this week. Not the trendy one on the main square, with the reclaimed-wood tables and the pour-over. The other one. The one on Fourth Street, next to the auto parts store, with the vinyl stools and the coffee that has been on the burner since six.

Do not read your phone.

Watch the woman behind the counter.

Watch her pour the coffee before the cup is empty. Watch her not pour the coffee when the couple in the corner booth are having the conversation they need to have. Watch her lead the widowed man to the side of the counter that does not face the booth where he used to sit. Watch her remember the order.

Then, on Monday morning, before you open the CRM — before the segment builder suggests a two-hundred-and-fourteen dollar ask on the sixteenth line down — pull down a plain notecard from the top drawer, and write, in your own hand, the small careful sentence Denise would have written if she were paid, in this life, to write it.

Sign it.

Mail it from your kitchen table on Saturday morning, so the postmark reads the same town your donor's grandmother is buried in.

The coffee is still on the burner.

The cup is still on the counter.

Denise has been keeping it warm since 1994. ♡
